 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Principle",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The LT1026 does not regulate. One charge pump doubles the positive input, and a second pump inverts that doubled voltage, giving a positive and a negative output from one supply. Both outputs move with the input voltage and with load, and a load on either output lowers the voltage and the current left for the other. The outputs deliver up to 15 mA. Built on a bipolar process, it cannot latch up, and it can produce as much as 36 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitors",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Four capacitors are all it needs: C1 and C2 pump, and one storage capacitor sits on each output. 1 µF is the nominal value. Down to 0.1 µF still works, with poorer performance; 22 µF cuts ripple and brings the output impedance down a little."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Paralleling",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "For more output current, several LT1026s can share common output capacitors with their outputs tied together."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Substrate",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The substrate is connected to -VOUT, and its isolation diodes must stay reverse biased at all times, overloads included. When only the doubled output is used, -VOUT goes to ground. If -VOUT can be pulled above ground, an external diode from the substrate to ground is needed so the substrate never conducts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Short circuits",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "There is no overload protection. A brief short on either output does no harm; a short that lasts draws enough current to overheat the chip. The absolute maximum short-circuit duration is 10 s."
  }
 ],
